h which basically meant that you know  the army was afraid it might lose its  grip on power um and one man was pretty  clear he said you know the  the army wants to turn the entire  country into a military base  meaning that you know the army wants to  take control of the whole country  and not cede any power to to elected  civilians or elected politicians  naomi how is the army able to wield so  much influence in myanmar  so the army myanmar is basically a very  powerful state within the myanmar state  if we look very briefly at the history  of myanmar basically myanmar has been  governed  governed by the military for most of its  recent  history since it gained independence  from the uk made it from britain  basically and in that time the army  managed to amass great wealth so  land economic and financial power  for a long time it was basically  impossible to do business in myanmar  without  going through the army or affiliated  businesses of the army  and what they've also done is you know  established their own court system  they're not  accountable to the civilian courts they  run their own universities  so a very powerful yet very opaque  state within the estate that's basically  accountable to no one  what did these defectors tell you did  they provide a glimpse into the life  of an average soldier in myanmar  they did they did and it's a very  cloistered a very strictly monitored  life i mean they were very clear that  the army has a facebook monitoring team  that you know  looks at all their social media activity  and also hauls people in that you know  maybe  voice their support for the national  league for democracy or criticize  uh the government there's also a lot of  propaganda um  officers have to take part in a  three-month training course which you  know  uh paints the army is the savior's the  defender of myanmar against the muslims  there's a lot of anti-muslim rhetoric  that  soldiers are fed the three men that we  talked to they didn't necessarily  believe all this propaganda  um but they knew well enough that they  couldn't voice any criticism because  that would have been dangerous  and also uh right now um they're  basically  soldiers are basically living this this  life on the base that they're not  allowed to leave they need to ask their  superiors if they want to leave  they have to do all their shopping on  their bases their schools on the bases  so i think an average soldier you could  say has  very little to no contact with civilians  you did mention earlier that these three  defectors are in a dangerous  situation but for the moment being are  they safe  i wouldn't say they're safe so it seems  pretty clear that the army has drawn  up a list of wanted activists  and journalists and also of defectors  and uh they haven't published this  because  it's obviously embarrassing for them to  to publish how many people have have  left the army despite the the great  danger  but they're hunting down these people  those are putting a lot of pressure on  the defectors families i mean  one man told us that his parents  received threatening phone calls  basically telling them you know where's  your son if you don't tell us where your  son is then  we'll come and get you um at which point  he's severed all communication with his  family so they're in a very dangerous  situation they're  trying to leave the country um but they  know that even if they make it to  thailand or to india they're not  necessarily safe there i mean they could  also get  deported back it's a very complex  situation at the moment they don't  really know  how to stay safe other than you know  move from one safe house to the next  they've obviously thrown away the phones  they had when they were still on the  military base but  it's dangerous and they're trying to  remain safe naomi conrad thank you very  much  for your reporting
 

